<chapter id="mtrxszref">
#title("Linear Matrices-with-size")

#para("\
\
#emphasis("Note:") the first versions of this chapter described persistent
matrices-with-size (the type #stacode("mtrxszref")), which are reclaimed by
garbage collection (GC). That type was removed when the support for GC was
dropped: the compiler now rejects every nonlinear heap allocation. A
matrix-with-size is now a linear value, which has to be freed explicitly.
The prelude does not declare a type for it, but it is easy to define one
(see below).
\
")

#para("\

I use the name #emphasis("matrix-with-size") to refer to a matrix
with attached dimension information (that is, number of rows and
number of columns).  Given a viewtype VT, a matrix-with-size
that contains M rows and N columns of elements of viewtype VT is
essentially a record of three components of types
#stacode("matrixptr(VT, M, N)"), #stacode("size_t(M)") and
#stacode("size_t(N)"), where M and N are unknown statically. We declare a
linear (boxed) type for it, which we call #stacode("mtrxszptr"):

#dats2xhtml('\
datavtype
mtrxszptr(a:vt0p) =
  | {m,n:nat} MTRXSZPTR of (matrixptr(a, m, n), size_t(m), size_t(n))
')

")

#para('\

The following function is commonly used to create a matrix-with-size, and
the second function frees it:

#dats2xhtml('\
fun{a:t0p}
mtrxszptr_make_elt
  (m: size_t, n: size_t, x0: a): mtrxszptr(a) = let
//
val m = g1ofg0(m) and n = g1ofg0(n)
val () = assertloc(m >= 0 && n >= 0)
//
in
  MTRXSZPTR(matrixptr_make_elt<a>(m, n, x0), m, n)
end // end of [mtrxszptr_make_elt]

fun
mtrxszptr_free
  {a:t0p}
  (M: mtrxszptr(a)): void = let
  val ~MTRXSZPTR(M0, m, n) = M
in
  matrixptr_free(M0)
end // end of [mtrxszptr_free]
')

Given two sizes m and n plus an element x0, #dyncode("mtrxszptr_make_elt")
returns a matrix-with-size of the dimension m by n in which each matrix-cell
is initialized with the given element x0.\

')

#para('\

For accessing and updating the content of a matrix-cell, the following
two functions #dyncode("mtrxszptr_get_at") and #dyncode("mtrxszptr_set_at")
can be implemented. Here is the first of them:

#dats2xhtml('\
fun{a:t0p}
mtrxszptr_get_at
  (M: !mtrxszptr(a), i: size_t, j: size_t): (a) = let
//
val+ @MTRXSZPTR(M0, m, n) = M
val i = g1ofg0(i) and j = g1ofg0(j)
//
in
//
if i < m
  then (
    if j < n
      then let
        val x = matrixptr_get_at(M0, i, n, j)
        prval () = fold@(M)
      in
        x
      end // end of [then]
      else let
        prval () = fold@(M)
      in
        $raise MatrixSubscriptExn()
      end // end of [else]
  ) (* end of [then] *)
  else let
    prval () = fold@(M)
  in
    $raise MatrixSubscriptExn()
  end // end of [else]
//
end // end of [mtrxszptr_get_at]
')

The pattern #dyncode("@MTRXSZPTR(M0, m, n)") unfolds the value
#dyncode("M") in place, which gives access to its three components, and
#dyncode("fold@(M)") folds it back in each branch before the function
returns; the argument is borrowed (#keycode("!")) and thus still available
to the caller. Notice that matrix-bounds checking is performed at run-time
whenever #dyncode("mtrxszptr_get_at") or #dyncode("mtrxszptr_set_at") is
called, and the exception #dyncode("MatrixSubscriptExn") is raised in case
of out-of-bounds matrix access being detected. The test of the column
index is nested in the test of the row index so that the type-checker has
both facts at hand when the cell is accessed statically.
Writing #dyncode("mtrxszptr_set_at") is analogous (see the code of this
chapter).

')

#para('\

As a simple example, the following code implements a function that
transpose the content of the matrix inside a given matrix-with-size:

#dats2xhtml('\
extern
fun{a:t0p}
mtrxszptr_transpose
  (M: !mtrxszptr(a)): void
//
implement
{a}(*tmp*)
mtrxszptr_transpose
  (M) = let
//
val n = mtrxszptr_get_nrow<a>(M)
//
val () = assertloc(n = mtrxszptr_get_ncol<a>(M))
//
fun loop
(
 M: !mtrxszptr(a), n: size_t, i: size_t, j: size_t
) : void =
(
if
(j < n)
then let
  val x = mtrxszptr_get_at<a>(M, i, j)
  val y = mtrxszptr_get_at<a>(M, j, i)
  val () = mtrxszptr_set_at<a>(M, i, j, y)
  val () = mtrxszptr_set_at<a>(M, j, i, x)
in
  loop(M, n, i, succ(j))
end // end of [then]
else let
  val i1 = succ(i)
in
  if i1 < n then loop(M, n, i1, succ(i1)) else ()
end // end of [else]
//
) (* end of [loop] *)
//
in
  if (n > 0) then loop(M, n, i2sz(0), i2sz(1)) else ()
end // end of [mtrxszptr_transpose]
')

')

#para('\

Like arrays-with-size, matrices-with-size are easier to program with than
dependently typed matrices. However, the latter can not only lead to more
effective error detection at compile-time but also more efficent code
execution at run-time. For someone programming in ATS, it is quite
reasonable to start out with matrices-with-size and then replace them with
matrices when there are clear benefits from doing so.\

')

#para("\

Please find #mycodelink("chap_mtrxszptr.dats", "on-line") the entirety of the
code used in this chapter.\

")#comment("para")

</chapter><!--"mtrxszref"-->

